# Migrating a flat Pulumi TS program to `ComponentResource` — with zero replacements

## 0. Why anything moves at all

Pulumi identity is the **URN**, which is built from:

```
urn:pulumi:<stack>::<project>::<parentType$...>$<type>::<name>
```

Nothing about the cloud resource changes when you wrap it in a component — but the **parent type chain** does. A bucket that was a direct child of the root stack:

```
urn:...::assets-bucket::aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::app-assets
```

becomes, once parented to `MyOrg:app:Storage`:

```
urn:...::assets-bucket::myorg:app:Storage$aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::app-assets
```

Pulumi sees an unknown URN → **create**, plus the old URN unreferenced → **delete**. For an RDS instance or an EKS cluster that is a catastrophic replace. `aliases` tells the engine "this new URN *is* that old URN", so it does a state rewrite instead.

Two rules that carry the whole migration:

1. **Every resource that moves under a new parent needs `aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }]`** (its old parent was the implicit root stack).
2. **Aliases on a `ComponentResource` are inherited by its children.** That matters for *later* refactors (renaming the component, re-nesting components), not for the first flat→component hop, because the flat resources had no component parent to inherit from.

---

## 1. Before — the flat program (abridged)

`index.ts` (single file, everything at root):

```ts
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";
import * as eks from "@pulumi/eks";

const cfg = new pulumi.Config();
const env = pulumi.getStack();                 // "dev" | "prod"
const nodeCount = cfg.requireNumber("nodeCount");
const nodeType  = cfg.require("nodeInstanceType");
const dbClass   = cfg.require("dbInstanceClass");
const multiAz   = cfg.requireBoolean("dbMultiAz");
const dbPassword = cfg.requireSecret("dbPassword");

// ---- storage
const assets = new aws.s3.BucketV2("app-assets", { forceDestroy: false });
new aws.s3.BucketVersioningV2("app-assets-versioning", {
  bucket: assets.id, versioningConfiguration: { status: "Enabled" },
});
new aws.s3.BucketServerSideEncryptionConfigurationV2("app-assets-sse", {
  bucket: assets.id,
  rules: [{ applyServerSideEncryptionByDefault: { sseAlgorithm: "AES256" } }],
});
new aws.s3.BucketPublicAccessBlock("app-assets-pab", {
  bucket: assets.id, blockPublicAcls: true, blockPublicPolicy: true,
  ignorePublicAcls: true, restrictPublicBuckets: true,
});

// ---- database
const dbSubnets = new aws.rds.SubnetGroup("app-db-subnets", { subnetIds: /* ... */ [] });
const db = new aws.rds.Instance("app-db", {
  engine: "postgres", instanceClass: dbClass, allocatedStorage: 20,
  dbName: "app", username: "appadmin", password: dbPassword,
  multiAz, storageEncrypted: env === "prod",
  backupRetentionPeriod: 14, deletionProtection: true,
  publiclyAccessible: false, dbSubnetGroupName: dbSubnets.name,
  skipFinalSnapshot: false, finalSnapshotIdentifier: `app-db-final-${env}`,
});

// ---- cluster, identity, edge ... (same shape)
const cluster = new eks.Cluster("app-cluster", { /* ... */ });

export const bucketName = assets.bucket;
export const dbEndpoint = db.address;
```

---

## 2. After — components with aliases

### 2.1 A tiny helper

```ts
// src/alias.ts
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";

/** Resource used to live directly under the stack (flat program). */
export const wasFlat: pulumi.Alias[] = [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }];

/** Same, but the logical name also changed during the move. */
export const wasFlatNamed = (oldName: string): pulumi.Alias[] => [
  { parent: pulumi.rootStackResource, name: oldName },
];
```

> `pulumi.rootStackResource` is the canonical way to say "old parent was the stack itself". Do **not** use `{ parent: undefined }` — that means "no change to parent" and is a no-op.

### 2.2 Storage component

```ts
// src/storage.ts
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";
import { wasFlat } from "./alias";

export interface StorageArgs {
  env: "dev" | "prod";
  replicaRegion?: string;                 // prod only
  replicationRoleArn?: pulumi.Input<string>;
}

export class Storage extends pulumi.ComponentResource {
  public readonly bucket: aws.s3.BucketV2;
  public readonly bucketName: pulumi.Output<string>;

  constructor(name: string, args: StorageArgs, opts?: pulumi.ComponentResourceOptions) {
    super("myorg:app:Storage", name, {}, opts);
    const parent = { parent: this };

    // NOTE: physical names below are the *flat* names. Keeping the logical
    // name identical is what makes `wasFlat` sufficient (no `name:` needed).
    this.bucket = new aws.s3.BucketV2("app-assets",
      { forceDestroy: false },
      { ...parent, aliases: wasFlat });

    new aws.s3.BucketVersioningV2("app-assets-versioning",
      { bucket: this.bucket.id, versioningConfiguration: { status: "Enabled" } },
      { ...parent, aliases: wasFlat });

    new aws.s3.BucketServerSideEncryptionConfigurationV2("app-assets-sse",
      { bucket: this.bucket.id,
        rules: [{ applyServerSideEncryptionByDefault: { sseAlgorithm: "AES256" } }] },
      { ...parent, aliases: wasFlat });

    new aws.s3.BucketPublicAccessBlock("app-assets-pab",
      { bucket: this.bucket.id, blockPublicAcls: true, blockPublicPolicy: true,
        ignorePublicAcls: true, restrictPublicBuckets: true },
      { ...parent, aliases: wasFlat });

    if (args.env === "prod" && args.replicaRegion && args.replicationRoleArn) {
      // NEW resource — no alias, it never existed before.
      new aws.s3.BucketReplicationConfig("app-assets-replication", {
        bucket: this.bucket.id,
        role: args.replicationRoleArn,
        rules: [{ id: "assets", status: "Enabled", priority: 1,
                  deleteMarkerReplication: { status: "Enabled" },
                  filter: {},
                  destination: { bucket: /* replica bucket arn */ "", storageClass: "STANDARD_IA" } }],
      }, parent);
    }

    this.bucketName = this.bucket.bucket;
    this.registerOutputs({ bucketName: this.bucketName });
  }
}
```

### 2.3 Database component

```ts
// src/database.ts
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";
import { wasFlat } from "./alias";

export interface DatabaseArgs {
  env: "dev" | "prod";
  instanceClass: pulumi.Input<string>;
  multiAz: pulumi.Input<boolean>;
  password: pulumi.Output<string>;        // ConfigSecret
  subnetIds: pulumi.Input<string[]>;
  vpcSecurityGroupIds: pulumi.Input<string[]>;
}

export class Database extends pulumi.ComponentResource {
  public readonly instance: aws.rds.Instance;
  public readonly connectionString: pulumi.Output<string>;

  constructor(name: string, args: DatabaseArgs, opts?: pulumi.ComponentResourceOptions) {
    super("myorg:app:Database", name, {}, opts);
    const parent = { parent: this };

    const subnets = new aws.rds.SubnetGroup("app-db-subnets",
      { subnetIds: args.subnetIds },
      { ...parent, aliases: wasFlat });

    this.instance = new aws.rds.Instance("app-db", {
      engine: "postgres", engineVersion: "16.3",
      instanceClass: args.instanceClass, allocatedStorage: 20,
      dbName: "app", username: "appadmin", password: args.password,
      multiAz: args.multiAz,
      storageEncrypted: args.env === "prod",
      backupRetentionPeriod: 14,
      deletionProtection: true,
      publiclyAccessible: false,
      dbSubnetGroupName: subnets.name,
      vpcSecurityGroupIds: args.vpcSecurityGroupIds,
      skipFinalSnapshot: false,
      finalSnapshotIdentifier: `app-db-final-${args.env}`,
    }, {
      ...parent,
      aliases: wasFlat,
      protect: true,                       // belt & braces during the migration
      ignoreChanges: ["password"],         // avoid drift noise on the alias update
    });

    this.connectionString = pulumi.secret(
      pulumi.interpolate`postgresql://appadmin:${args.password}@${this.instance.address}:5432/app`);

    this.registerOutputs({ endpoint: this.instance.address });
  }
}
```

`Cluster`, `Identity` (IAM user for dev / OIDC role for prod), and `Edge` (ACM + CloudFront/ALB) follow the identical pattern: `super("myorg:app:X", ...)`, `{ parent: this, aliases: wasFlat }` on every pre-existing child, no alias on genuinely new resources.

### 2.4 New `index.ts`

```ts
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import { Storage } from "./src/storage";
import { Database } from "./src/database";
import { Cluster } from "./src/cluster";
import { Identity } from "./src/identity";
import { Edge } from "./src/edge";

const cfg = new pulumi.Config();
const env = pulumi.getStack() as "dev" | "prod";

const network = /* existing vpc lookup, also aliased if it moved */;

const cluster = new Cluster("app", {
  env,
  desiredSize: cfg.requireNumber("nodeCount"),
  instanceType: cfg.require("nodeInstanceType"),
  subnetIds: network.privateSubnetIds,
});

const identity = new Identity("app", { env, oidcProviderArn: cluster.oidcProviderArn });

const storage = new Storage("app", {
  env,
  replicaRegion: cfg.get("replicaRegion"),
  replicationRoleArn: identity.replicationRoleArn,
});

const database = new Database("app", {
  env,
  instanceClass: cfg.require("dbInstanceClass"),
  multiAz: cfg.requireBoolean("dbMultiAz"),
  password: cfg.requireSecret("dbPassword"),
  subnetIds: network.privateSubnetIds,
  vpcSecurityGroupIds: [network.dbSecurityGroupId],
});

const edge = new Edge("app", { env, originBucket: storage.bucket, domain: cfg.require("domain") });

// Outputs must keep the same NAMES as before, or downstream stack references break.
export const bucketName = storage.bucketName;
export const dbEndpoint = database.instance.address;
export const kubeconfig = pulumi.secret(cluster.kubeconfig);
export const cdnDomain  = edge.domainName;
export const dbConnectionString = database.connectionString;  // secret-marked
```

### 2.5 Stack configs (unchanged by the refactor)

`Pulumi.dev.yaml`
```yaml
config:
  aws:region: us-east-1
  app:nodeCount: "2"
  app:nodeInstanceType: t3.medium
  app:dbInstanceClass: db.t3.micro
  app:dbMultiAz: "false"
  app:domain: dev.example.internal
  app:dbPassword:
    secure: AAABAG5s...          # ConfigSecret, encrypted at rest
```

`Pulumi.prod.yaml`
```yaml
config:
  aws:region: us-east-1
  app:nodeCount: "4"
  app:nodeInstanceType: t3.large
  app:dbInstanceClass: db.t3.medium
  app:dbMultiAz: "true"
  app:replicaRegion: us-west-2
  app:domain: app.example.com
  app:dbPassword:
    secure: AAABAJ2x...
```

---

## 3. Verification: prove there are zero replacements

```bash
# 1. Snapshot state first — this is the rollback.
pulumi stack export --stack dev > dev-before.json
pulumi stack export --stack prod > prod-before.json

# 2. Preview on dev.
pulumi preview --stack dev --diff
```

Expected preview shape:

```
Type                            Name              Plan
pulumi:pulumi:Stack             assets-bucket-dev
+   myorg:app:Storage           app               create      <- virtual, no cloud call
+   myorg:app:Database          app               create
+   myorg:app:Cluster           app               create
+   myorg:app:Identity          app               create
+   myorg:app:Edge              app               create

Resources:
    + 5 to create          (component resources only)
    ~ 0 to update
    +-0 to replace
    - 0 to delete
    23 unchanged
```

The only `create` lines are the `ComponentResource`s themselves — they are engine-side constructs with no provider behind them. **If you see any `+-` (replace), `-` (delete), or a `create` of an `aws:*`/`eks:*` type, an alias is missing or wrong.** Stop and fix before applying.

Machine-checkable gate for CI:

```bash
pulumi preview --stack prod --json > plan.json
jq -e '
  [ .steps[]
    | select(.op == "replace" or op == "delete" or op == "delete-replaced"
             or (.op == "create" and (.urn | contains("$") ) and (.newState.type | test("^(aws|eks|kubernetes):"))))
  ] | length == 0
' plan.json || { echo "REPLACEMENT DETECTED — aborting"; exit 1; }
```

Then apply dev first, confirm, then prod:

```bash
pulumi up --stack dev
pulumi up --stack prod
```

---

## 4. Troubleshooting the aliases you got wrong

| Symptom in preview | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `+ aws:s3/bucket:Bucket app-assets` **and** `- aws:s3/bucket:Bucket app-assets` | No alias on that child | add `aliases: wasFlat` |
| Still creating/deleting despite `wasFlat` | Logical name changed when you moved it (e.g. `"app-assets"` → `"assets"`) | `aliases: wasFlatNamed("app-assets")` |
| `~ update` with a `bucket:` / physical-name diff | You let Pulumi auto-name inside the component; the old physical name was explicit (or vice versa) | pin the physical name, or `ignoreChanges` if provider allows |
| Replace on RDS with `provider` in the diff | Component received an explicit `provider` that root didn't have | pass the same provider, or add `{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource, ... }` alias with matching stack/project |
| Alias ignored entirely | Used `{ parent: undefined }` | use `pulumi.rootStackResource` |
| Two components claim the same alias | Copy-pasted `wasFlat` onto a genuinely new resource with a colliding name | remove the alias from the new resource |

Cross-project/stack move (rare here) needs the long form:

```ts
aliases: [{ name: "app-db", type: "aws:rds/instance:Instance",
            project: "old-project", stack: "dev",
            parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }]
```

---

## 5. Alternative: `pulumi state move` (no code aliases)

Pulumi ≥ 3.130 can rewrite URNs directly:

```bash
pulumi state rename --stack dev \
  'urn:pulumi:dev::assets::aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::app-assets' ...
```

I do **not** recommend it as the primary path here: it's imperative, it isn't reproducible for the second environment without re-running by hand, and it leaves no record in the repo of why the URNs changed. Aliases are declarative and apply identically to `dev` and `prod`. Keep `state rename` as the recovery tool if a preview surfaces a replace you can't express as an alias.

---

## 6. Cleaning up the aliases afterwards

Once `pulumi up` has succeeded on **both** stacks, the state files record the new URNs and the old ones are gone. The aliases are then dead weight — but they are *cheap* dead weight and they are load-bearing for any stack you forgot about (an ephemeral PR stack, a DR stack, someone's local stack).

Policy I'd apply:

1. Land the refactor with aliases in place.
2. Run `pulumi stack ls --all` and apply to every stack derived from this project.
3. Only after all are updated, open a follow-up PR deleting `src/alias.ts` and the `aliases:` options — with the diff reviewed as "no-op if and only if all stacks migrated".
4. If in doubt, leave them. A stale alias costs nothing; a missing one costs a database.

Also drop the temporary `protect: true` / `ignoreChanges: ["password"]` in that follow-up if they weren't part of your steady-state posture.

---

## 7. What this refactor did *not* change

Worth stating explicitly, because it's the acceptance bar:

- Bucket: versioning on, AES-256 SSE, full public-access block — same resources, same URNs.
- RDS: `deletionProtection: true`, `backupRetentionPeriod: 14` (≥7), `publiclyAccessible: false`, encrypted + multi-AZ on prod only.
- Secret: `dbPassword` stays a Pulumi `ConfigSecret` (`secure:` in `Pulumi.<stack>.yaml`); the derived connection string is wrapped in `pulumi.secret()` so it stays encrypted in state and masked in outputs.
- IAM: dev = IAM user with programmatic access, prod = OIDC-trusted role, no wildcard actions on prod.
- Cluster: managed node groups, 2×t3.medium (dev) / 4×t3.large (prod) from stack config.
- HTTPS: 443 listener with ACM cert; no forced 80→443 redirect.
- Environment isolation: two independent stacks, two independent state files. `pulumi up --stack prod` cannot touch dev state — the refactor is applied per-stack and previewed per-stack.

The component boundary is a *code* organization change. The whole point of the alias work is that it is provably not an *infrastructure* change.
